 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 30,
   "plaque": "liverpool_chinese_arch",
   "title": "The Chinatown Arch",
   "text": "Chinese seamen began settling by the docks after Alfred Holt's Blue Funnel Line started sailing between Liverpool and China in 1866, and Liverpool's Chinatown is one of the oldest in Europe. The great arch on Nelson Street was put up in 2000 by craftsmen from Shanghai, Liverpool's twin city since 1999.",
   "people": "chinese"
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 17,
   "plaque": "liverpool_chinese_seamen",
   "title": "The Chinese Seamen",
   "text": "In the Second World War thousands of Chinese merchant seamen sailed from Liverpool and kept Britain supplied. In 1945 and 1946 the government quietly shipped hundreds of them back to China, many without a chance to say goodbye to the Liverpool wives and children they left behind. A memorial plaque at the Pier Head, unveiled in 2006, remembers them.",
   "people": "chinese"
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 30,
   "plaque": "liverpool_wootton",
   "title": "Charles Wootton",
   "text": "In June 1919, with work scarce after the war, white mobs attacked Black sailors and their families across Liverpool. Charles Wootton, a young ship's fireman who had served in the Royal Navy, was chased into Queens Dock and drowned. A centre for adult education in the city was later named after him.",
   "people": "black_britain"
  },
  {
   "x": 55,
   "y": 47,
   "plaque": "liverpool_lilian_bader",
   "title": "Lilian Bader",
   "text": "Lilian Bader was born in Liverpool in 1918, the daughter of a seaman from Barbados who had served in the First World War. In 1941 she joined the Women's Auxiliary Air Force, one of the first Black women in the British armed forces, trained as an instrument repairer and was made an acting corporal.",
   "people": "black_britain"
  },
  {
   "x": 60,
   "y": 15,
   "plaque": "liverpool_1847",
   "title": "Black '47",
   "text": "In 1847, the worst year of the Great Famine, around 300,000 people landed in Liverpool from Ireland. Some went on to America, but many stayed, crowded into courts and cellars near the docks, and Liverpool became one of the most Irish cities anywhere. Plenty of Scousers can trace a line back to that crossing.",
   "people": "irish"
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "FERRY TERMINAL. Sail from the Mersey to the Isle of Man. Bring a sandwich. And a sailor's hat if you've got one."
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "FERRY TO DOUGLAS. Departures when the tide, the captain and the seagulls agree."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "THE FAB FOUR, in bronze. Please do not ask them to get back together. They've heard."
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "SUPERLAMBANANA. It's exactly what it says. A banana-lamb. Don't think too hard."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "THE LIVER BIRDS. If they fly away, Liverpool ceases to exist. They have a very good union."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "ALBERT DOCK, 1846. The first warehouses built with no wood at all. Only brick, iron and stone. The fire brigade were overjoyed."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "THE CAVERN (ISH). Where four lads once played 292 times. Our drummer plays only 291. Keeper's Club. Members only. And you."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 35,
   "text": "LIME STREET. Beautiful station. Terrible at timetables. Excellent at goodbyes."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "LIVERPOOL CATHEDRAL. Biggest in Britain. Took 74 years to finish. Builders apparently on a tea break."
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 42,
   "text": "SEFTON PARK-ISH. Dunes, ducks and a man throwing a ball for a very tired dog."
  },
  {
   "x": 60,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "EAST: Chat Moss and Manchester. Scouse to Manc: 'You're a long way from the sea'."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 52,
   "text": "SOUTH: the Wirral Way and Chester. Mind the tide."
  },
  {
   "x": 1,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "Mersey Tunnel closed for maintenance. Ferry 'cross the Mersey instead, as the song says."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: The Beatles played at the Cavern Club in Mathew Street from 1961 to 1963, and the rest is history.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "liverpool_cavern_club",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Cavern Club"
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 15,
   "plaque": "liverpool_name",
   "title": "Name: Liverpool",
   "text": "Liverpool comes from Old English 'lifer', thick or muddy water, and 'pol', a pool or creek: the muddy pool, an inlet off the Mersey that was later filled in and built over. The Liver Bird came much later and takes its name from the city, not the other way round. Do not tell Bella and Bertie."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 31,
   "plaque": "liverpool_two_clubs",
   "title": "Two Clubs, One Ground",
   "text": "Everton were the first club to play at Anfield. In 1892, after a row with their landlord, John Houlding, they moved across Stanley Park to Goodison Park. Houlding, left with an empty ground, started a new club to fill it: Liverpool FC. Families have been arguing at Christmas ever since."
  },
  {
   "x": 59,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "liverpool_slavery",
   "people": "black_britain",
   "title": "Liverpool and Slavery",
   "text": "In the 1700s Liverpool became Britain's leading slave-trading port. Its ships carried well over a million enslaved Africans across the Atlantic, and much of the city's early wealth came from that trade. The International Slavery Museum, here on the waterfront, tells their story."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 9,
   "plaque": "liverpool_mersey_wildlife",
   "title": "The Mersey Comes Back",
   "text": "In the 1980s the Mersey was called one of the most polluted rivers in Europe. After decades of clean-up, salmon swim back up it, and harbour porpoises and grey seals turn up off the waterfront. Not bad for a river that was once mostly sewage and old shoes."
  },
  {
   "x": 59,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "NORTH: the Dock Road to Crosby's iron men, Formby's squirrels and Southport's pier. Keep going and it's Blackpool, the Tower and the lights. Bring a coat. Bring two."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "arts_beatles",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Beatles",
   "text": "On 6 July 1957 a sixteen-year-old John Lennon played the St Peter's Church fete in Woolton with his skiffle group, the Quarrymen, and was introduced to a fifteen-year-old Paul McCartney. George Harrison joined the next year. As the Beatles they first played the Cavern Club on Mathew Street on 9 February 1961, Ringo Starr arrived in 1962, and 'Love Me Do' came out that October. They stopped touring in 1966 and split in 1970, having changed what a pop record could be. Liverpool has not stopped talking about it, and sees no reason to start."
  }
 ],
